name: memory
intro: Local variables live on the stack: made when a function is
intro: entered, gone when it returns. Returning a pointer to one is
intro: undefined behaviour -- the address outlives the data. When data
intro: must outlive the function, or its size is not known until runtime,
intro: ask the heap with `malloc` (from `<stdlib.h>`).
intro:
intro:     int *v = malloc(n * sizeof *v);
intro:     if (v == NULL) { /* out of memory: say so, exit 1 */ }
intro:     /* ... use v[0] .. v[n-1] ... */
intro:     free(v);
intro:
intro: `sizeof *v` keeps the size right if the type changes. Check EVERY
intro: allocation: malloc returns NULL when it fails, and writing through
intro: NULL is undefined. `calloc(n, size)` zeroes the block and refuses
intro: an n * size that overflows; with malloc you check it yourself:
intro: `if (n > SIZE_MAX / sizeof *v)` fail. A wrapped multiply gives a
intro: small block that you then write far past -- a classic exploit.
intro:
intro: Every malloc needs exactly one free, by whoever owns the block.
intro: Decide who owns it and write it in a comment. Using a block after
intro: free, or freeing it twice, is undefined behaviour -- it often
intro: seems to work until it corrupts something else.
intro:
intro: To grow a buffer, double it with `realloc`, through a TEMPORARY:
intro:
intro:     char *tmp = realloc(buf, cap * 2);
intro:     if (tmp == NULL) { free(buf); /* fail */ }
intro:     buf = tmp;
intro:
intro: Write `buf = realloc(buf, ...)` and a failure overwrites buf with
intro: NULL: the old block leaks and you crash. Doubling keeps the total
intro: copying small. `qsort(base, n, size, cmp)` sorts any array; cmp
intro: gets two `const void *` and returns <0, 0 or >0. A `const void *` is
intro: a pointer to anything; cast it to what it really points at:
intro:
intro:     static int cmp(const void *a, const void *b)
intro:     {
intro:         long x = *(const long *)a;
intro:         long y = *(const long *)b;
intro:         return (x > y) - (x < y);
intro:     }
---
q: rep.c -- repeat a string
spec: Takes TEXT and a count N and prints TEXT repeated N times, then a
spec: newline: `rep ab 3` prints `ababab`. Build it in memory from
spec: malloc, print it, free it.
spec:
spec: TEXT repeated N times (not counting the newline) may be at most
spec: 1048576 bytes. Larger prints `rep: too big`; an N that is not a whole
spec: number, is negative or does not fit a long prints `rep: bad count`;
spec: both to standard error, exit 1. N is a long and the length a size_t:
spec: cast N after you know it is not negative. Check the size with a
spec: division, not a multiply that could wrap. Empty TEXT prints an empty line for any valid N.
spec: Any other argument count prints nothing and exits 2.
file: rep.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: #include <string.h>
prog: 
prog: #define MAXOUT 1048576
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char *argv[])
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc != 3)
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 
prog: 	char *end;
prog: 	errno = 0;
prog: 	long n = strtol(argv[2], &end, 10);
prog: 	if (errno != 0 || end == argv[2] || *end != '\0' || n < 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "rep: bad count\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 
prog: 	const char *text = argv[1];
prog: 	size_t len = strlen(text);
prog: 	if (len > 0 && (unsigned long)n > MAXOUT / len) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "rep: too big\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 
prog: 	size_t total = len * (size_t)n;
prog: 	char *out = malloc(total + 1);
prog: 	if (out == NULL) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "rep: out of memory\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	for (long i = 0; i < n && len > 0; i++)
prog: 		memcpy(out + (size_t)i * len, text, len);
prog: 	out[total] = '\0';
prog: 	printf("%s\n", out);
prog: 	free(out);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: rep ab 3
case: rep x 0
case: rep '' 1000000000
case: rep abcd 262144 | wc -c
case: rep abcd 262145 2>&1; echo $?
case: rep ab -1 2>&1; echo $?
case: rep ab; echo $?
hint: total is len * n plus one for the NUL; refuse before multiplying if n is bigger than the limit divided by len
